===Conjunction===
{{en-head|conj}}

# {{non-gloss|Introducing a clause that is the object of a verb, especially a [[reporting verb]] or verb expressing belief, knowledge, perception, etc.}}
#: {{ux|en|He told me '''that''' the book is a good read.}}
#: {{ux|en|I believe '''that''' it is true.''}}
#: {{ux|en|I can see '''that''' the ladder won't reach.''}}
#* {{RQ:en:CNN|author=Bu Kerry Chan Laddaran|title=Pidgin English now an official language of Hawaii|url=https://www.cnn.com/2015/11/12/living/pidgin-english-hawaii|date=2015-11-12|passage=Pidgin is a combination of expressions and phrases '''that''' are recognizable to those who speak it. To non-Pidgin speakers, it may sound like slang. For example, “dat” means that and “fadda” means father or dad.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260411051658/https://www.cnn.com/2015/11/12/living/pidgin-english-hawaii|archivedate=11 April 2026}}
# {{non-gloss|Introducing a clause that is the subject of a verb, especially the 'be' verb or a verb expressing judgement, opinion, etc.}}
## {{non-gloss|As delayed subject.}}
##: {{ux|en|It is almost certain '''that''' she will come.}}
##: {{ux|en|It amazes me '''that''' people still believe this nonsense.}}
## {{lb|en|chiefly|_|literary}} {{non-gloss|As direct subject.}}
##: {{ux|en|'''That''' she will come is almost certain.}}
##: {{ux|en|'''That''' people still believe this nonsense amazes me.}}
# {{non-gloss|Introducing the complement of a mandative verb.}}
#: {{ux|en|I insist '''that''' you come.}}
#: {{ux|en|The boss ordered '''that''' the worker enter the room.}}
# {{non-gloss|Introducing a clause that complements an adjective or [[passive participle]].}}
#: {{ux|en|I'm sure '''that''' you are right.}}
#: {{ux|en|She is convinced '''that''' he is British.}}
## {{non-gloss|Expressing a reason or cause:}} [[because]], [[in that]].
##: {{ux|en|Be glad '''that''' you have enough to eat.}}
##: {{ux|en|I was saddened '''that''' you felt so little for me.}}
# {{non-gloss|Introducing a [[subordinate clause]] modifying an adverb.}}
#: {{ux|en|Was John there? — Not '''that''' I saw.}}
#: {{ux|en|How often did she visit him? — Twice '''that''' I saw.}}
#* {{RQ:Trollope The Claverings|passage="{{...|Tell her from me," Lady Ongar had said, "that|nospace=1}} I will go anywhere '''that''' she may wish if she will go with me,"}}
# {{sid|en|preceding noun}} {{non-gloss|Introducing a clause that describes the information content of a preceding reporting noun.}}
#: {{ux|en|I heard a rumour '''that''' they got married.}}
#: {{ux|en|Reports '''that''' he left the country are circulating.}}
# {{non-gloss|Introducing – especially, but not exclusively, with an antecedent like ''[[so]]'' or ''[[such]]'' – a [[subordinate clause]] expressing a result, consequence{{,}} or effect.}}
#: {{ux|en|The noise was so loud '''that''' she woke up.}}
#: {{ux|en|The problem was sufficiently important '''that''' it had to be addressed.}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|author=w:Zoe Williams|title=I swore I wouldn't be an embarrassing mother. But the key to being a success in life is changing your mind, right?|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20140814182542/https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2008/may/23/familyandrelationships.women|date=23 May 2008
|passage=My dad apparently always said that no child of his would ever be harassed for its poor eating habits, and then I arrived, and I was so disgusting '''that''' he revised his opinion.}}
# {{lb|en|dated}} {{non-gloss|Introducing a [[subordinate clause]] that expresses an aim, purpose{{,}} or goal ("final"), and usually contains the auxiliaries ''[[may]]'', ''[[might]]''{{,}} or ''[[should]]'':}} [[so]], [[so that]], [[in order that]].
#: {{ux|en|He fought '''that''' others might have peace.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merchant of Venice Q1|act=I|scene=iii|sig=B2|verso=1|page=32|text=''Baſſ''[''anio'']. Be aſſured you may. / ''Shy''[''lock'']. I will be aſſured I may: and '''that''' I may be aſſured, I will bethinke me, may I ſpeake with ''Anthonio''?}}
#* {{RQ:Pope Rape of the Lock|360|text=When hungry Judges ſoon the Sentence ſign, / And Wretches hang '''that''' Jury-men may Dine; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1833|magazine=Parley's Magazine|volume=1|page=23
|passage=Ellen's mamma was going out to pay a visit, but she left the children a large piece of rich plumcake to divide between them, '''that''' they might play at making feasts.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1837|journal=The Edinburgh New Philosophical Journal|volume=23|page=222
|passage='''That''' he might ascertain whether any of the cloths of ancient Egypt were made of hemp, M. Dutrochet has examined with the microscope the weavable filaments of this last vegetable.}}
#* {{RQ:Browning Poems|edition=new|volume=II|poem=Sonnets from the Portuguese|sonnet=XIV|page=451|passage={{...}}Since one might well forget to weep who bore / Thy comfort long, and lose thy love thereby. / But love me for love's sake, '''that''' evermore / Thou may'st love on through love's eternity.}}
#* {{RQ:Asbjornsen Braekstad Tales|chapter=Legends of the Mill|page=156|passage=In the olden days people had a stronger belief in all kinds of witchery; now they pretend not to believe in it, '''that''' they may be looked upon as sensible and educated people, as you say.}}
#* {{RQ:R. F. Burton Arabian Nights|supp=yes|volume=III|chapter=Alaeddin; or, The Wonderful Lamp|night=547|page=108|passage=Now one day of the days,{{...}}the Sultan cast his eyes upon her as she stood before him, and said to his Grand Wazir, "This be the very woman whereof I spake to thee yesterday, so do thou straightway bring her before me, '''that''' I may see what be her suit and fulfil her need."}}
# {{lb|en|archaic|or|poetic}} {{non-gloss|Introducing a premise or supposition for consideration:}} seeing as; inasmuch as; given that; as would appear from the fact that.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Comedy of Errors|act=III|scene=ii|page=91|column=2|text=What are you mad, '''that''' you doe reaſon ſo?}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Tale of Two Cities|chapter=The Period|page=1|text=[I]n short, the period was so far like the present period, '''that''' some of its noisiest authorities insisted on its being received, for good or for evil, in the superlative degree of comparison only.}}
#* {{circa|1911}}, {{w|D.H. Lawrence}}, third draft of what became ''{{w|Sons and Lovers}}'', in Helen Baron (editor), ''Paul Morel'', Cambridge University Press (2003), {{ISBN|978-0-521-56009-2}}, [http://books.google.com/books?id=fpHGg5qeawEC&pg=PA234&dq=that page 234]:
# {{lb|en|archaic|or|poetic}} {{non-gloss|Introducing an exclamation expressing a desire or wish.}}
